{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I will harden Pharaoh's heart.</b> Many ask here: if the Holy One, blessed be He, hardened his heart so that he would not send them, why did He bring upon him all those great plagues for nothing — and this would appear to be injustice, whereas the Blessed Name is righteous and does no wrong. And if the hardness of heart and the refusal to send them came from Him and from Himself, how could He punish him for his hardness of heart with all those plagues? The answer to this is that Pharaoh was wicked on another account and had already sinned. If his sin had been what prevented him from sending Israel, and the Holy One, blessed be He, strengthened his heart in that prevention and then punished him for it, that would be injustice. But his sin is explicit in the verse, for he said (Exodus 1): ״הנה עם בני ישראל רב ועצום ממנו״ — 'Behold, the people of Israel are more numerous and mightier than us' — ״הבה נתחכמה לו״. Since he was wicked and an unbeliever of his own accord and acted maliciously against them, he and his people, he became liable to have the ways of repentance withheld from him — that is, the Holy One, blessed be He, placed in his heart that he would not send them; for had they repented, it would be impossible to punish them, as proven by the men of Nineveh. Therefore it was necessary to withhold from them the ways of repentance, and He said: ״ואני אקשה את לב פרעה״. Thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״ואני אקשה את לב פרעה״ — Rabbi Yohanan said: from here is an opening for the sectarians to say that he had no power to repent. Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said: their mouths shall be stopped. Rather, 'if to the scorners He scorns' — He warns him once, twice, a third time, and he does not turn back, then He locks the door of repentance against him in order to exact from him payment for what he sinned. So too Pharaoh the wicked: once the Holy One, blessed be He, sent to him five times and he paid no heed to His words, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: you have stiffened your neck and made your heart heavy — I will add further impurity to your impurity." "<b>And according to</b> this Midrash, the meaning of ״ואני אקשה את לב פרעה״ will be: at the end. For in the first five plagues it says only ״ויחזק לב פרעה״ and ״ויכבד פרעה את לבו״ — and he did not wish to send them, for the glorification of the Name, blessed be He. But when the plagues overpowered him and he grew weary of bearing them, his heart softened and he considered sending them because of the severity of the plagues — not to do the will of his Creator. However, in the latter five plagues it says: ״ויחזק ה' את לב פרעה״, ״כי הקשה ה' את לבו״, למען ספר שמו בכל הארץ — as it is written: ״והתגדלתי והתקדשתי״, etc." "<b>And know</b> that this hardening of the heart was already hinted to Moses at the beginning of his prophecy, in the section of the bush. That is what is written (Exodus 3): ״וירא והנה הסנה בער באש והסנה איננו אכל״. Scripture likened the multiplicity of troubles and plagues to fire, and likened Pharaoh the wicked to a thorn — which is the bush (סנה). And it was hinted to Moses from this what the Holy One, blessed be He, was destined to do in hardening Pharaoh's heart so that he would not be broken by the plagues, just as the bush is not consumed by the fire. And this is what He said to him from within this vision of the bush: ״ועתה לכה ואשלחך אל פרעה״, as I wrote there."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:49.714734345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}